   "pitches": 8
  },
  {
   "approach": "Take the climbers' trail from the highway up to the base of the south face. Start by scrambling easy slabs right of the start of Yak Crack.",
   "area_id": "bc_yak_peak",
   "descent": null,
   "descent_text": "The final pitch is a fourth-class slab to trees, and from there you can scramble easily upward or rappel. Walking off is faster and safer than rappelling. From the trees, continue through an opening to the shoulder of the false summit, pass the false summit on its right by a short fourth-class ledge, and pick up the main trail. A steep snow patch can sit at the top of the descent; if you would rather avoid it, bolted rappel anchors on a small wall below the trail drop into the meadow. Follow the trail down through the meadow bowl and along the east flank, back to the foot of the south face.",
   "description": null,
   "discipline": "trad",
   "grade": "5.11a R",
   "id": "bc_yak_peak_reality_check",
   "name": "Reality Check",
   "need_approach": true,
   "need_descent": false,
   "parent": "Anderson River Group",
   "peak": "Yak Peak",
   "peak_elev": null,
   "peak_lat": 49.6067,
   "peak_lng": -121.10389,
   "pitches": 12
  },
  {
   "approach": "The approach is the same as for Speedway: reach the base of the slabs and cross toward the middle of the southwest face. The route follows the long gully on the left edge of the slabby south face; go up the floor of it and bear right where it forks.",
